1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,640
Your AirPods hearing test just came back looking fine?

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:05,680
Here's what to check before you trust that result completely.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:16,400
Apple's Hearing Aid feature got FDA clearance as the first over-the-counter hearing aid software, and a clinical study found self-fit users did about as well as those fitted by a professional.

4
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:23,360
Still, three things are worth knowing: who it's for, what the test can't catch, and all-day wear.

5
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:27,680
First, it's cleared only for adults with mild-to-moderate hearing loss.

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:34,880
Severe loss, and any sudden or one-sided loss, are explicitly excluded — the software isn't built for those cases.

7
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:37,760
Second, there's no professional fitting behind it.

8
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:44,560
You calibrate it yourself through an in-app tone test, so adjustments a real audiologist would normally catch can get missed.

9
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:48,560
Finally, it's not built for all-day wear the way a real hearing aid is.

10
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:55,120
Battery runs around six hours, and leaving earbuds in your ear canal for hours raises the risk of irritation.

11
00:00:55,440 --> 00:01:01,520
If a hearing test comes back showing sudden or one-sided loss, don't just turn up the amplification and move on.

12
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:07,040
See an ENT doctor first — catching the actual cause early is what protects your hearing.
